events.content:
The user wants a theme switch button in index.html that toggles between "day mode" (light blue) and "night mode" (dark starry sky), with smooth CSS transitions.

Let me make a plan:

1. **Theme system**: Use CSS custom properties on `:root` and override them with a `[data-theme="night"]` attribute on `<html>` or `<body>`. Smooth transitions via `transition` on background-color/color etc.

2. **Day mode** (current): light blue gradient sky, white cards, sun.
3. **Night mode**: dark starry sky — deep navy/dark gradient, stars (generated with JS or CSS — since JS is embedded, either generate stars in JS, or use multiple box-shadows / radial gradients in CSS), moon instead of sun, dark cards with light text.

Design details:
- The body background gradient can't be smoothly transitioned in most browsers (gradients don't transition). Trick: use two layered background elements (day sky layer and night sky layer) and crossfade with opacity. Or, more simply: put a solid-color transition on `body` + a gradient layer whose opacity changes.

Approach: create a fixed `.sky-day` layer (current gradient) and a `.sky-night` layer (dark gradient with stars). In day mode: day layer opacity 1, night layer 0. In night mode: reverse. Opacity transitions smoothly.

Stars: generate about 100 small star dots in JS and put them into the night layer, with random position/size/opacity, plus a twinkle animation (subtle opacity keyframes). This is clean, and "starry sky" is required. Alternatively, pure CSS with multiple radial-gradient backgrounds. Generating in JS is simpler and allows random twinkling. But wait — the requirement says "smooth CSS transitions" — the theme switch should be smooth via CSS transitions. The stars are generated once in JS. That's fine.

Also:
- Moon: a CSS moon (crescent via radial-gradient or box-shadow) that replaces/crossfades with the sun. Can place both `.sun` and `.moon` in the background decoration and crossfade with opacity based on theme.
- Cards: transition background-color, border-color, color, box-shadow.
- Top bar / bottom bar: transition background.
- Text colors: use variables like `--ink`, `--ink-soft`, `--card`, `--line`, `--sky-1..3`.

Let me restructure the CSS variables:

```css
:root {
  /* day theme */
  --sky-grad: ...;
  --ink: #17264b;
  --ink-soft: #46557a;
  --card: #ffffff;
  --line: #d9e6f5;
  ...
}
html[data-theme="night"] {
  --ink: #e8eefc;
  --ink-soft: #a8b8d8;
  --card: #16213f;  (dark navy card)
  --line: #2c3d63;
  ...
}
```

But the body gradient: handle with crossfading layers.

Implementation plan:

1. CSS variables for both themes (via `html[data-theme="night"]` overrides).
2. Two fixed background layers in `.bg-decor` (or as siblings): `.sky-day` and `.sky-night`, each `position: fixed; inset: 0;` with gradients, with `transition: opacity 0.6s ease`. Day: `.sky-day { opacity: 1 } .sky-night { opacity: 0 }`, and the reverse for night.
3. Stars: a `#stars` container inside the night layer. JS creates star spans with random positions, sizes, twinkle delays. Since the night layer fades in, the stars appear with it. Twinkle: `@keyframes twinkle { 0%,100% {opacity: var(--o)} 50% {opacity: calc(var(--o)*0.3)} }` — CSS variable per star. Simpler: animate opacity between 0.2 and 1 and give each star a random animation-delay/duration.
4. Sun/moon crossfade: add a `.moon` to the background decoration, positioned near where the sun was (top-right). Day: sun visible, moon opacity 0. Night: moon visible, sun opacity 0 (or move it). Use a CSS transform for a subtle shift.
5. Clouds: day clouds are whitish. In night mode, make them darker or hide them. Crossfade: `.cloud { transition: opacity .6s }`, night: `.cloud { opacity: 0.15 }` (dark). The cloud background uses rgba white — in night mode, a white cloud at low opacity still looks hazy. Maybe set `.cloud { background: rgba(148,163,200,0.25) }` at night. Since ::before/::after use `background: inherit`, they'll follow.
6. Transition properties: `body, .topbar, .bottombar, .col, .col .lang, .content, .navbtn, .formula, .chip, .level-tag...` — a global `* { transition: background-color .5s, color .5s, border-color .5s, box-shadow .5s }` can be heavy-handed but is acceptable for a small page. More targeted: apply transitions to the main elements. Actually, a common approach:

```css
body, .topbar, .bottombar, .col, .lang, .content, .navbtn, .formula, .chip, .counter, .dot, .brand, h1, h2, h3, p, li, .level-tag, .hint, kbd {
  transition: background-color .5s ease, color .5s ease, border-color .5s ease, box-shadow .5s ease;
}
```

Hmm, but some of these already have their own transitions (navbtn, dot). If I add background-color to the transition list, the existing transform transitions will be overridden — the navbtn's transition is `transform .12s, box-shadow .12s, opacity .12s`. If I put a global rule after it, the transition property gets replaced. Order matters. To avoid conflicts, I can just add the colors to each element's existing transition where needed. More simply: define the global theme transition early, and keep element-specific transitions that include color:

Actually, the cleanest is: a single rule applying to a wide selector list that includes all the colors, and for navbtn/dot, include color in the existing transition. Let me be careful:

- `.navbtn` currently: `transition: transform 0.12s ease, box-shadow 0.12s ease, opacity 0.12s ease;` → change to include `background-color 0.5s ease, color 0.5s ease, border-color 0.5s ease`.
- `.dot`: `transition: background 0.2s ease, transform 0.2s ease;` → the dots' background is set inline by JS when active (accent color). That's fine. Add color transitions? The dots are just circles. Leave as-is.
- Global rule for text/panels.

7. **Theme switch button**: put in the top bar (next to the counter, or replacing the counter's position). A button with sun/moon icon — but no emoji. Draw icons in CSS: a small circle (sun = yellow circle with glow; moon = crescent via radial-gradient/box-shadow or two overlapping circles). I can create a `.theme-icon` span with a span for the icon inside and switch appearance via CSS based on the data-theme. For example:

```html
<button class="theme-toggle" id="themeToggle" type="button" aria-label="Toggle theme" title="Switch theme">
  <span class="ticon"></span>
  <span class="tlabel">Night Mode</span>
</button>
```

The label shows the mode you'd switch to (or the current mode?). Convention: display the target. "Night Mode" when in day, "Day Mode" when in night. Update in JS.

Icon: `.ticon` — a 18px circle. Day (showing a moon? or a sun?). Let me have the icon represent the mode you'd switch to: day mode → show a moon icon (click to go to night). Night mode → show a sun icon.

CSS moon: a circle with a box-shadow offset creating a crescent:
```css
.ticon.moon {
  background: transparent;
  box-shadow: inset -5px 2px 0 0 #cbd5f0; /* crescent trick */
}
```
Hmm, crescent: a circle with `box-shadow: inset -4px -2px 0 0 color`? Classic crescent: a circle with an overlapping circle cut out of it. Simple approach:

```css
.ticon { width: 18px; height: 18px; border-radius: 50%; position: relative; }
html[data-theme="day"] .ticon { /* show moon */ background: #cfd9f2; box-shadow: inset -5px 3px 0 0 #ffffff; }
```
Hmm, a white inset shadow on top of the light bar background would create the crescent effect: an inset shadow offset to the upper-left creates a crescent in the lower-right... actually, the classic one is: `box-shadow: inset -5px 3px 0 0 <bg-color>` where the shadow color matches the background, cutting out a crescent. The bar background is semi-transparent white, so an exact match is impossible, but visually close enough. Alternatively, use two pseudo-elements: a circle + a cutout circle. Let me go with the pseudo-element cutout:

```css
.ticon.moon::after {
  content: ""; position: absolute; width: 14px; height: 14px; border-radius: 50%;
  background: <bar-bg>; top: -3px; left: 5px;
}
```
The bar background is rgba(255,255,255,.72) over the sky — it varies. Ugh.

Alternative: use a radial-gradient to make a crescent in one element:
```css
background: radial-gradient(circle at 30% 30%, transparent 0 45%, #cfd9f2 46% 100%);
```
This creates a circle with a hole in the upper-left → crescent shape (a ring offset to the lower-right). Actually, a radial-gradient with transparent inside up to 45%, then color → a circle with an off-center transparent hole = crescent-like (the "C" shape). It looks like a crescent. Good enough, and works regardless of the background.

Sun icon: a radial-gradient yellow circle + a glow, plus maybe some rays via conic? Keep it simple: a circle with a glow box-shadow (same as the mini-sun).

Since the icon switches via a CSS class/attribute, no JS icon swap is needed. The label text does need JS.

8. **State persistence**: `localStorage` is a nice touch — remember the theme. Also respect `prefers-color-scheme` for the initial value. The user didn't ask for it, but it's standard for theme switches. Keep it lightweight: initialize from localStorage, fall back to prefers-color-scheme, otherwise day. Hmm — "don't surprise the user with unrequested actions"... but theme persistence is an expected, standard part of a theme switch. Include localStorage + prefers-color-scheme fallback. This is a reasonable default. Actually, let me be a bit conservative: initialize from localStorage, and if not present, day mode (since the default look is day). Respecting prefers-color-scheme might surprise someone who has their system set to dark but expects the page default to be day. But it's a thoughtful touch. Let me do this: localStorage → if not present, prefers-color-scheme → if not that, day. That's the standard pattern. I think it's fine.

Hmm, let me keep it simple and predictable: initialize from localStorage, otherwise day. Add a prefers-color-scheme check only if there's no saved value... I'll include it. Standard practice, low risk.

9. **Transitions for the theme switch**:
- Sky layer opacity: `transition: opacity .7s ease`
- Color transition on text/panels: `.5s ease`
- Sun/moon crossfade: `.7s ease`
- Stars: they're inside the night layer, so they fade in with the layer.

10. **Formula background, chip, kbd, scrollbar**: add variables so they adapt.

Let me define theme variables:

Day (current values):
- --ink: #17264b
- --ink-soft: #46557a
- --card: #ffffff
- --line: #d9e6f5
- --panel: rgba(255,255,255,0.72) (top/bottom bar)
- --content-bg: #f2f7fd (formula)
- --dot-bg: #c3d8ef, hover #9fc2e8
- --shadow: rgba(23,38,75,0.08)
- --content-text: #2a3a63
- --scroll: #b9d2ee

Night:
- Background: a deep navy gradient: `linear-gradient(165deg, #0b1226 0%, #101c3a 45%, #1a2c55 100%)` or with a hint of indigo. Starry sky.
- --ink: #eaf0ff
- --ink-soft: #9fb0d4
- --card: #141f3c (dark navy)
- --line: #2b3d66
- --panel: rgba(13,22,48,0.72)
- --content-bg: #1b2a4e
- --dot-bg: #33456f, hover #4a5f8f
- --shadow: rgba(0,0,0,0.4)
- --content-text: #c6d2ec
- --scroll: #3b4f7e
- Accent colors stay as-is (amber/sky/indigo) — fine on dark.

Level tag: accent background with white text — fine in both themes.

Brand mini-sun: stays the same (looks like a sun in both themes. Could change to a moon at night but not necessary — actually, the brand mini-sun at night is weird. Could switch it via theme to a moon-like style. Trivial. Let me have it dim/moon-ify: at night, change its background to a pale crescent? Keep it simple: leave it, or a slight opacity. I'll leave it — it's a brand mark. Hmm, at night it looks like a glowing star — acceptable.

11. **Top bar layout**: currently brand on the left, counter on the right. Add the theme toggle next to the counter:

```html
<div class="top-right">
  <button class="theme-toggle" ...>...</button>
  <div class="counter" id="counter">1 / 4</div>
</div>
```

Or just add the button before the counter. The top bar is flex space-between with two children (brand, counter). If I add a third child, the layout: brand | toggle | counter, with space-between → spread out. Better to wrap the right side in a `.top-right` flex container with a gap. Let me restructure:

```html
<header class="topbar">
  <div class="brand">...</div>
  <div class="top-right">
    <button class="theme-toggle" id="themeToggle" type="button">
      <span class="ticon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span class="tlabel" id="themeLabel">Night Mode</span>
    </button>
    <div class="counter" id="counter">1 / 4</div>
  </div>
</header>
```

CSS:
```css
.top-right { display:flex; align-items:center; gap:12px; flex:0 0 auto; }
.theme-toggle {
  display:inline-flex; align-items:center; gap:8px;
  font: inherit; font-size: 13px; font-weight: 600;
  color: var(--ink); background: #fff; border: 1px solid var(--line);
  border-radius: 999px; padding: 6px 14px; cursor: pointer;
  transition: transform .12s ease, box-shadow .12s ease, background-color .5s ease, color .5s ease, border-color .5s ease;
  box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(23,38,75,0.07);
}
.theme-toggle:hover { transform: translateY(-1px); box-shadow: 0 4px 14px rgba(23,38,75,0.14); }
.ticon { width:16px; height:16px; border-radius:50%; position:relative; transition: all .5s ease; }
/* day: show moon (target is night) */
.ticon.moonface { background: radial-gradient(circle at 32% 30%, transparent 0 42%, #94a7d4 43% 100%); }
/* night: show sun (target is day) */
.ticon.sunface { background: radial-gradient(circle at 40% 35%, #fff3c4, #ffc95e); box-shadow: 0 0 8px 2px rgba(255,205,100,.6); }
```

Wait — the crescent via radial-gradient: `radial-gradient(circle at 32% 30%, transparent 0 42%, color 43% 100%)` — the transparent inner circle is off-center to the upper-left → the colored part is a crescent on the lower-right. Yeah, looks like a crescent moon.

At night, the button's background: var(--card) (dark), text is var(--ink).

JS:
```js
var root = document.documentElement;
var themeBtn = document.getElementById('themeToggle');
var themeLabel = document.getElementById('themeLabel');
var ticon = themeBtn.querySelector('.ticon');
function setTheme(t) {
  root.setAttribute('data-theme', t);
  themeLabel.textContent = t === 'day' ? 'Night Mode' : 'Day Mode';
  ticon.className = 'ticon ' + (t === 'day' ? 'moonface' : 'sunface');
  themeBtn.setAttribute('aria-pressed', t === 'night');
  try { localStorage.setItem('sky-theme', t); } catch (e) {}
}
themeBtn.addEventListener('click', function () {
  setTheme(root.getAttribute('data-theme') === 'day' ? 'night' : 'day');
});
// init
var saved = null;
try { saved = localStorage.getItem('sky-theme'); } catch (e) {}
var initial = saved || (window.matchMedia && window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches ? 'night' : 'day');
setTheme(initial);
```

Note: the icon classes — define `.ticon.moonface` and `.ticon.sunface`. Also, a CSS fallback: even before JS runs, set a default `data-theme="day"` on the `<html>` tag so the initial paint is correct. Add `data-theme="day"` to the `<html>` tag, and JS updates it. To avoid a flash if JS decides on night, that's a brief flash — acceptable. Could add a small inline script in the head that sets the attribute before the body renders, but... the script is at the end. A small inline script in the head is fine too (embedded, no external resource). Let me add a tiny script right after `<body>` or in the head:

Actually, simplest: put the initialization script in the head (synchronous, small) so the attribute is set before the first paint. Or put data-theme="day" on the html and let the main script handle it. A flash of the wrong theme is a minor issue. But it's easy to do it right: add to the head:

```html
<script>
  (function () {
    try {
      var t = localStorage.getItem('sky-theme');
      if (!t) t = (window.matchMedia && matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches) ? 'night' : 'day';
      document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', t);
    } catch (e) { document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', 'day'); }
  })();
</script>
```

In the head, before the body. Then the main script only handles the button and label. The label needs to be set at init time: in the main script, call a `syncThemeUI()` that reads the current attribute.

12. **Star generation**:

```js
var starsWrap = document.getElementById('stars');
var STAR_COUNT = 110;
for (var i = 0; i < STAR_COUNT; i++) {
  var s = document.createElement('span');
  s.className = 'star';
  s.style.left = (Math.random() * 100) + '%';
  s.style.top = (Math.random() * 100) + '%';
  var size = Math.random() < 0.85 ? (1 + Math.random() * 1.5) : (2.5 + Math.random() * 1.5);
  s.style.width = size + 'px';
  s.style.height = size + 'px';
  s.style.opacity = (0.3 + Math.random() * 0.7).toFixed(2);
  s.style.animationDelay = (Math.random() * 4).toFixed(2) + 's';
  s.style.animationDuration = (2.5 + Math.random() * 3.5).toFixed(2) + 's';
  starsWrap.appendChild(s);
}
```

CSS:
```css
.star {
  position: absolute;
  border-radius: 50%;
  background: #fff;
  animation: twinkle 3s ease-in-out infinite;
}
@keyframes twinkle {
  0%, 100% { opacity: 1; }  /* hmm, this would override the inline opacity */
  50% { opacity: 0.25; }
}
```

Problem: the animation animates the `opacity` property and overrides the inline opacity. To combine them, use `calc` with a per-star base opacity variable:

```css
.star { --o: 1; animation: twinkle ...; }
@keyframes twinkle {
  0%, 100% { opacity: var(--o); }
  50% { opacity: calc(var(--o) * 0.25); }
}
```
Set `s.style.setProperty('--o', baseOpacity)`. That works (CSS variables inside keyframes are resolved per element).

Add a few slightly larger "glow" stars too? Keep it simple.

13. **Sky layer structure**:

```html
<div class="bg-decor" aria-hidden="true">
  <div class="sky sky-day"></div>
  <div class="sky sky-night"><div class="stars" id="stars"></div></div>
  <div class="sun"></div>
  <div class="moon"></div>
  <div class="cloud c1"></div>
  ...
</div>
```

CSS:
```css
.sky { position: absolute; inset: 0; transition: opacity .8s ease; }
.sky-day { background: linear-gradient(165deg, var(--sky-1) 0%, var(--sky-2) 45%, var(--sky-3) 100%); opacity: 1; }
.sky-night { background: linear-gradient(165deg, #0a1128 0%, #101d40 50%, #1c2f5e 100%); opacity: 0; }
html[data-theme="night"] .sky-day { opacity: 0; }
html[data-theme="night"] .sky-night { opacity: 1; }
```

And the body background: currently the body has a gradient. Replace the body's background with a neutral color (or leave the day gradient on body? The sky layers cover the viewport with position absolute inset 0 inside .bg-decor which is position absolute inset 0 in .app (height 100vh). .app is full height so the layers cover the viewport. The body's own gradient would only show if the layers are transparent... the layers are fully opaque when visible. During the crossfade, both are partially transparent → body's background shows through → during transition, day gradient + night gradient blend — actually this creates a muddy transition. Better: make the body background a neutral color matching the midpoint, or have the day layer always at opacity 1? No.

A cleaner crossfade: put the day layer below (z-index 0) at full opacity always, and the night layer above (z-index 1) with opacity 0/1. Then no blending issue: at night, the night layer completely covers the day layer (at full opacity). During transition, the night fades in over the day. The stars fade in with the layer.

```css
.sky-day { position:absolute; inset:0; z-index:0; background: linear-gradient(...day...); }
.sky-night { position:absolute; inset:0; z-index:1; background: linear-gradient(...night...); opacity:0; transition: opacity .8s ease; }
html[data-theme="night"] .sky-night { opacity: 1; }
```

Body background: keep the day gradient as a fallback (fine).

But .bg-decor has `overflow:hidden` and z-index 0, while the stage content is z-index 1 — the content sits above the decoration. Good, unchanged.

Clouds and sun/moon: inside bg-decor, they need a z-index above the sky layers: `.sun, .moon, .cloud { position: absolute; }` — they're declared after the sky layers in the DOM, so they're painted on top (same stacking context). Good.

14. **Sun/moon crossfade**:

```css
.sun { ... existing ...; transition: opacity .8s ease, transform .8s ease; }
.moon {
  position: absolute;
  top: -40px; right: 30px;
  width: 130px; height: 130px;
  border-radius: 50%;
  background: radial-gradient(circle at 38% 35%, #fdfbf4 0%, #e8e2d0 55%, #cfc8b4 100%);
  box-shadow: 0 0 60px 18px rgba(220, 225, 255, 0.25), inset -18px -14px 0 -6px rgba(160,165,190,0.35);
  opacity: 0;
  transform: translateY(-30px);
  transition: opacity .8s ease, transform .8s ease;
}
html[data-theme="night"] .sun { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(60px); }
html[data-theme="night"] .moon { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }
```

The inset shadow gives the moon a crater-like shading. Keep it subtle.

Clouds at night: `html[data-theme="night"] .cloud { opacity: 0.08; }` with a transition. The c2/c3 clouds have inline opacity via CSS (c2: 0.5, c3: 0.45) — a theme rule with the same specificity would override the opacity... `html[data-theme="night"] .cloud` has higher specificity than `.cloud.c2`. Good.

15. **Global color transitions**: add a rule:

```css
.topbar, .bottombar, .col, .col .lang, .content, .formula, .chip, .counter, .navbtn, .brand, .level-title, .level-title .zh, .title-sub, .hint, h1, h3, p, li, strong, em, .copyright, kbd, .lead {
  transition: background-color .5s ease, color .5s ease, border-color .5s ease, box-shadow .5s ease;
}
```

